{"status":"ok","message-type":"work","message-version":"1.0.0","message":{"indexed":{"date-parts":[[2025,12,1]],"date-time":"2025-12-01T15:38:10Z","timestamp":1764603490552},"reference-count":19,"publisher":"Wiley","issue":"1","license":[{"start":{"date-parts":[[2019,2,8]],"date-time":"2019-02-08T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1549584000000},"content-version":"am","delay-in-days":365,"URL":"http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/termsAndConditions#am"},{"start":{"date-parts":[[2018,2,8]],"date-time":"2018-02-08T00:00:00Z","timestamp":1518048000000},"content-version":"vor","delay-in-days":0,"URL":"http://onlinelibrary.wiley.com/termsAndConditions#vor"}],"funder":[{"DOI":"10.13039/501100000781","name":"European Research Council","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["278295"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["278295"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039/501100000781","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"DOI":"10.13039/100000001","name":"National Science Foundation","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["DMS‐1412529"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["DMS‐1412529"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039/100000001","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]},{"DOI":"10.13039/501100001602","name":"Science Foundation Ireland","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","award":["16/IA/4443","16/SPP/3347","08/SRC/FMC1389"],"award-info":[{"award-number":["16/IA/4443","16/SPP/3347","08/SRC/FMC1389"]}],"id":[{"id":"10.13039/501100001602","id-type":"DOI","asserted-by":"publisher"}]}],"content-domain":{"domain":[],"crossmark-restriction":false},"short-container-title":["Mathematical Finance"],"published-print":{"date-parts":[[2019,1]]},"abstract":"<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title><jats:p>When trading incurs proportional costs, leverage can scale an asset's return only up to a maximum multiple, which is sensitive to its volatility and liquidity. In a model with one safe and one risky asset, with constant investment opportunities and proportional costs, we find strategies that maximize long‐term returns given average volatility. As leverage increases, rising rebalancing costs imply declining Sharpe ratios. Beyond a critical level, even returns decline. Holding the Sharpe ratio constant, higher asset volatility leads to superior returns through lower costs.</jats:p>","DOI":"10.1111/mafi.12172","type":"journal-article","created":{"date-parts":[[2018,2,8]],"date-time":"2018-02-08T05:28:38Z","timestamp":1518067718000},"page":"249-284","source":"Crossref","is-referenced-by-count":7,"title":["The limits of leverage"],"prefix":"10.1111","volume":"29","author":[{"given":"Paolo","family":"Guasoni","sequence":"first","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Mathematics and Statistics Boston University Boston MA USA"},{"name":"School of Mathematical Sciences Dublin City University Dublin Ireland"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]},{"given":"Eberhard","family":"Mayerhofer","sequence":"additional","affiliation":[{"name":"Department of Mathematics &amp; Statistics University of Limerick Limerick Ireland"}],"role":[{"role":"author","vocabulary":"crossref"}]}],"member":"311","published-online":{"date-parts":[[2018,2,8]]},"reference":[{"key":"e_1_2_9_2_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.2469/faj.v68.n1.1"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_3_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1007/978-3-0348-8163-0"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_4_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1086/261410"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_5_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1137/0311020"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_6_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1287/moor.15.4.676"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_7_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1111/j.1540-6261.1991.tb02675.x"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_8_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1257/jep.13.2.189"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_9_1","doi-asserted-by":"crossref","unstructured":"Frazzini A. &Pedersen L. H.(2012).Embedded leverage. Technical Report National Bureau of Economic Research.","DOI":"10.3386/w18558"},{"key":"e_1_2_9_10_1","doi-asserted-by":"publisher","DOI":"10.1016/j.jfineco.2013.10.005"},{"ke